  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nA closed low along the coast briefly will be cutoff from the\nmain steering flow along the US/Canada border, eventually\nmeandering across our area through Saturday. This will bring an\nincreasing chance of showers and thunderstorms starting\ntomorrow, including overnight thunderstorm potential. Through\ntomorrow morning, since storms will be more dry vs decent rain\nproducers, there is a 10-50% chance for gusts over 35 mph,\nhighest across the southeast highlands. For tomorrow, there is a\n40-70% chance for gusts over 35 mph across the Snake Plain,\nMagic Valley, and South Hills/Albion Mountains...with peak gusts\napproaching 50 mph. Lower chances for those types of gusts are\nexpected elsewhere. The track of the low will put most of the \nprecipitation across the eastern highlands, Lemhi County, and \nnorth into southwest Montana. Storm totals outside of those \nareas will be anywhere from a couple of hundredths to near 0.30\"\nover 72-ish hours. Locally higher amounts are possible across \nthe southeast highlands and portions of the central mountains.\nWhere heavier precipitation occurs, we are looking at\n0.30-0.75\", with locally higher amounts along the Montana border\nand the Tetons. We wouldn't be surprised to see a rain/snow mix\non the highest peaks when the low crosses Friday night/Saturday\nmorning. Ahead of the low, temperatures will top out in the\nupper 70s to lower 80s tomorrow for lower elevations. Highs\nFriday and Saturday will only manage the mid 60s to mid 70s for\nthe valleys both days. \n\nWe will likely see a few wraparound showers on the backside of\nthat low for Sunday, with dry conditions to start out next week.\nTemperatures warm back up slightly, close to 80 for the warmest\nspots. We will need to watch the middle of next week so if the\nstorm track shifts back to the north enough to bring showers and\nstorms back into eastern Idaho.",
